## TL;DR  
MapLibre is a **v1-branch** of Mapbox GL JS.  
The “camera” API that lets you pass an **`altitude`** (meters above the ground) was added in Mapbox GL JS v2 and has **never been implemented in MapLibre**.  
So after the migration the property is simply ignored, which is why the map “breaks” (or just stays at the default zoom).

To get the same visual result you have to:

1. **Drop the `altitude` field** – MapLibre only understands `center`, `zoom`, `pitch`, `bearing`, and `padding`.
2. **Convert the altitude you used in v2 to a zoom level** (or vice-versa) using the Mercator-scale formula.
3. Use the standard view-setting methods (`new maplibregl.Map({center, zoom, …})`, `map.flyTo()`, `map.jumpTo()`, …).

Below is a complete guide, including the math, a ready-to-copy migration snippet, and a few gotchas you might run into.

---

## 1. What changed between the two libraries?

| Feature | Mapbox GL JS v2 | MapLibre (v1-branch) |
|---------|----------------|----------------------|
| **Free-camera API (`setFreeCameraOptions`, `camera` option)** | ✅ Supports `position: {center, altitude, bearing, pitch}` | ❌ Not implemented |
| **`altitude` (meters above ground)** | ✅ Part of the free-camera model | ❌ Ignored – only `zoom` is used |
| **Terrain & 3-D** | ✅ Works with `terrain` source + free-camera | ✅ Works with `terrain` source **but** you still control the view with `zoom` (no true altitude) |
| **Map options** | `camera` can be passed at construction | Only `center`, `zoom`, `pitch`, `bearing`, `padding` are accepted |

> **Bottom line:** MapLibre can display 3-D terrain, but you control *how far* you are from the ground with **zoom**, not with an explicit altitude value.

---

## 2. Converting altitude ↔️ zoom

The relationship between altitude (in metres) and zoom level is derived from the Web-Mercator projection:

\[
\text{scale} = \frac{2^{\text{zoom}}}{\text{world\_size\_in\_pixels}} = \frac{2^{\text{zoom}}}{256}
\]

\[
\text{groundResolution} = \frac{\cos(\phi) \times 2\pi R}{256 \times 2^{\text{zoom}}}
\]

where  

* \(\phi\) = latitude (radians)  
* \(R = 6 378 137\) m (Earth’s radius)  

Altitude is essentially the distance from the camera to the ground measured **along the line of sight**. For a nadir view (pitch = 0) the simple approximation used by Mapbox is:

\[
\text{altitude} \approx \frac{\cos(\phi) \times 2\pi R}{256 \times 2^{\text{zoom}}}
\]

Re-arranged to solve for **zoom**:

\[
\boxed{\text{zoom} = \log_2\!\Bigl(\frac{\cos(\phi) \times 2\pi R}{256 \times \text{altitude}}\Bigr)}
\]

> **Note:** This formula assumes a *straight-down* view (pitch = 0). If you tilt the camera (pitch > 0) the apparent altitude changes, but MapLibre still only uses `zoom`. In practice you’ll usually keep the same pitch you had in the v2 code and just recompute the zoom.

### Helper functions (plain JavaScript)

```js
// Earth radius in metres (WGS-84)
const EARTH_RADIUS = 6378137;

// Convert altitude (m) → zoom (number)
function altitudeToZoom(altitude, latitude) {
  const phi = latitude * Math.PI / 180; // rad
  const numerator = Math.cos(phi) * 2 * Math.PI * EARTH_RADIUS;
  const zoom = Math.log2(numerator / (256 * altitude));
  return zoom;
}

// Convert zoom → altitude (m) – handy for debugging
function zoomToAltitude(zoom, latitude) {
  const phi = latitude * Math.PI / 180;
  const altitude = Math.cos(phi) * 2 * Math.PI * EARTH_RADIUS / (256 * Math.pow(2, zoom));
  return altitude;
}
```

---

## 3. Migration recipe

### 3.1. Original Mapbox GL JS v2 snippet (simplified)

```js
const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'mapbox://styles/your/style',
  camera: {
    center: [12.4924, 41.8902],   // lon, lat (Rome)
    altitude: 500,                // metres above ground
    bearing: 30,
    pitch: 45
  }
});
```

### 3.2. Equivalent MapLibre code

```js
// 1️⃣ Compute the zoom that corresponds to the old altitude
const latitude  = 41.8902;
const altitude  = 500;               // metres (same value you used before)
const zoomLevel = altitudeToZoom(altitude, latitude);

// 2️⃣ Build the MapLibre map – note the *absence* of `camera`
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // or your own style URL
  center: [12.4924, latitude],
  zoom: zoomLevel,
  bearing: 30,
  pitch: 45,
  // optional: preserve the same padding you had before
  // padding: {top: 0, bottom: 0, left: 0, right: 0}
});

// 3️⃣ If you need to change the view later, use the same conversion:
function setView({center, altitude, bearing, pitch}) {
  const newZoom = altitudeToZoom(altitude, center[1]);
  map.easeTo({
    center,
    zoom: newZoom,
    bearing,
    pitch
  });
}
```

That’s it – the map will appear at roughly the same “height” you had in the v2 version.

---

## 4. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Map stays at world-zoom (z ≈ 0)** | You passed `camera: { … }` to the MapLibre constructor. MapLibre silently drops the whole object because it’s not a recognized option. | Remove `camera` from the constructor; use `center` + `zoom` (or `flyTo`/`jumpTo`). |
| **Pitch or bearing ignored** | You set them inside `camera` instead of top-level options. | Move `pitch` and `bearing` to the root of the options object (or call `map.easeTo({pitch, bearing})`). |
| **Altitude changes after a `flyTo`** | You called `map.flyTo({center, altitude, …})`. MapLibre’s `flyTo` does **not** accept `altitude`. | Replace `altitude` with a pre-computed `zoom`. |
| **Terrain looks “flat” after migration** | You kept the old `terrain` source but forgot to add the `terrain` property to the style, or you’re using a style that disables the `terrain` layer. | Ensure your style JSON contains a `terrain` source and that the `terrain` property is set (`map.setTerrain({source: 'mapbox-dem', exaggeration: 1})`). |
| **Zoom jumps unexpectedly when you tilt the map** | You’re using a *fixed* zoom derived from altitude at pitch = 0, but then you change the pitch. The visual distance to the ground changes, making the view feel “closer” or “farther”. | Either keep the pitch constant, or recompute the zoom each time you change the pitch (use the same formula, but multiply the altitude by `1 / Math.cos(pitchRad)`). |

---

## 5. Optional: Emulating a true free-camera (if you really need altitude)

If your app truly needs a **free-camera** that can move up/down while keeping the same pitch (e.g., a flight simulator), you have two practical options:

1. **Wrap MapLibre in a three-js scene** – render the map as a texture on a plane and let three-js handle the camera. This gives you full 3-D freedom but adds a lot of complexity.
2. **Patch MapLibre** – the upstream MapLibre repo has an open issue *“Add free-camera support”* (see https://github.com/maplibre/maplibre-gl-js/issues/1234). You can fork the library and implement `setFreeCameraOptions` yourself, but that’s a non-trivial engineering effort.

For most apps that only needed altitude to *position* the map initially, the **zoom-conversion** approach above is sufficient and far simpler.

---

## 6. Quick checklist for a smooth migration

| ✅ | Item |
|---|------|
| **✔️** | Remove any `camera` objects from `new maplibregl.Map({ … })`. |
| **✔️** | Convert every `altitude` → `zoom` using `altitudeToZoom`. |
| **✔️** | Pass `center`, `zoom`, `bearing`, `pitch` as **top-level** options. |
| **✔️** | Replace `map.flyTo({…, altitude})`, `map.jumpTo({…, altitude})`, `map.easeTo({…, altitude})` with the same calls **without** `altitude` (use `zoom`). |
| **✔️** | Verify that any terrain source is still referenced in your style JSON (`terrain: {source: 'dem', exaggeration: 1}`) |
| **✔️** | Test on a few different latitudes – the altitude-to-zoom conversion depends on latitude, so a single hard-coded zoom may look right at one place and too close/far at another. |
| **✔️** | If you used `setFreeCameraOptions` anywhere, replace
